Yes, I originally wrote such a thing for the record files back in 1993
(and have updated it for the current file format).
If you download the zip packet of my reclist utility from
http://www.netsonic.fi/~walker/nethack.html
you'll find the C source and msdos and os/2 executables of the mergrec
utility in the same packet.

I have never felt the need for any sorting in merging logfiles.. the only
use for which I combine logfiles is to extract statistics from them, and
in that case the order doesn't matter. Just put them together with cat or
copy, depending on your operating system.

> On a Unix system you have everything you need on board...
> cat record1 record2 ... recordN | sort -nr +1  > record.new
> sorts the N record files you listed numerically using the high score
> field.

That will merge all scores from those files, forgetting the
restriction that there should not be more than three entries for any
character name / class combination. My mergrec utility also takes care
about that.
Mergrec only merges two files on one run, but if you have more than two
files to merge, first merge two of them, then merge the result with the
third file and so on.

